# rules: java-xml-parser-left-at-its-defaults
# languages: java
# fixture: java/CWE-611.java
# from: CWE-611 (Improper Restriction of XML External Entity Reference), CWE-827
#
# Every JAXP factory resolves external entities by default, because that is
# what the XML specification asks for. So a document handed to an untouched
# DocumentBuilderFactory may say
# `<!ENTITY x SYSTEM "file:///etc/shadow">` and be answered, or name an
# http:// URL and make the parsing host fetch it. One default gives away a
# file read and a server-side request forgery together.
#
# Three rules in this corpus already report XXE, and all three report a
# program that turns a protection *off* - `setFeature(disallow-doctype-decl,
# false)`. Almost nobody writes that. The bug in the wild is the default, and
# its shape is a factory created and used with nothing said in between.
#
# XMLInputFactory is deliberately left out: xmlinputfactory-possible-xxe
# already asks this question of that one class, and two ids on one line is
# worse than one.
#
# Two conditions, and both are the method body read as text rather than a
# pattern - see "The function body as text". The method has to parse
# something, so that a factory built to be configured and handed back is not
# reported; and it must not mention hardening, so that a method which has had
# someone think about this is left alone even when the four calls it needs are
# inside a helper block or a try/catch for ParserConfigurationException.
#
# Coarse is the right resolution for the second test. The alternative is to
# name the exact features that suffice, which differs per parser and per JDK,
# and to report a programmer who hardened their parser slightly differently
# from the way the rule expected. That is the finding people learn to skip.
#
# A third condition arrived from Spring's test suite, which produced fifty-
# eight findings in four files: XML the program assembled in memory as a
# String is XML the program wrote, and an entity in it would have to have been
# put there by the program. `new StringReader(...)` and
# `new ByteArrayInputStream(...)` in the method body say that, and where they
# do the rule stops asking. What survives it, in the whole of
# spring-framework, is one factory in main source - the one Spring's own
# authors annotated with a comment explaining why they accepted it.
#
# What it costs: a factory created in one method and hardened in another is
# reported, and so is one hardened by a helper the rule cannot follow into.
# The name of the helper is usually `secure`- or `harden`-something, and those
# are in the regex for that reason.

["DocumentBuilderFactory.newInstance($$$_)",
 "SAXParserFactory.newInstance($$$_)",
 "TransformerFactory.newInstance($$$_)",
 "SchemaFactory.newInstance($$$_)",
 "XMLReaderFactory.createXMLReader($$$_)",
 "new SAXBuilder($$$_)",
 "new SAXReader($$$_)"] as $factories
| ["$RT $M($$$_) { $$$BODY }"] as $methods
| "\\.parse\\s*\\(|\\.transform\\s*\\(|newDocumentBuilder\\s*\\(|newSAXParser\\s*\\(|newTransformer\\s*\\(|newSchema\\s*\\(|newXMLReader\\s*\\(|\\.build\\s*\\(|\\.read\\s*\\(" as $parses
| "setFeature|setProperty|setXIncludeAware|setExpandEntityReferences|setEntityResolver|setAttribute|FEATURE_SECURE_PROCESSING|ACCESS_EXTERNAL|disallow-doctype-decl|(?i)harden|(?i)secure[A-Z_]" as $hardened
| "new StringReader\\s*\\(|new ByteArrayInputStream\\s*\\(|parseText\\s*\\(" as $inMemory
| scan_ast("*.java"; $factories + $methods) as $all
| ( $all | of($factories)
    | within($all | of($methods) | where_capture("BODY"; $parses))
    | outside($all | of($methods) | where_capture("BODY"; $hardened))
    | outside($all | of($methods) | where_capture("BODY"; $inMemory)) )
| finding("java-xml-parser-left-at-its-defaults";
    "this parser resolves external entities because nothing here told it not "
    + "to, so a document can make it read a local file or fetch a URL")
| report
